Umbrella employment lets a freelancer smooth pay across months instead of taking home the full invoice each time. While contracting at L'Oréal, I used that mechanism deliberately. I paid myself small salaries for six months, letting cash build up inside the umbrella company, then paid myself artificially high salaries for the next six months by releasing that reserve.

Banks calculate mortgage capacity from recent salary history, so those six inflated months qualified me for loans well above what my real freelance income would have supported. I bought two apartments with the difference. It was completely legal, just a lever almost no freelancer thinks to use.


